# The build context is deliberately narrow. Two reasons:
#
# 1. Correctness of CI change detection. The image is built from `COPY . .`, so anything
#    reaching the context becomes an input to the image. Excluding documentation is what
#    makes "a docs-only change cannot affect the container" a fact rather than a hope.
# 2. Security. Nothing here is published, but the same discipline applies: no VCS history,
#    no credentials, no local environment files ever enter a layer.

# Build output — built for a different RID on the host; would shadow the container's own.
**/bin/
**/obj/
**/TestResults/
**/coverage*.xml
**/*.trx

# Version control and CI definitions: not inputs to the build.
.git/
.gitignore
.github/

# Documentation: not compiled, not tested, not shipped.
*.md
docs/

# Container definitions themselves are read by the builder, not copied into the image.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml

# Editor and OS noise.
.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
*.user
*.suo
.DS_Store

# Local environment and secrets. None exist today; excluded so none can ever leak in.
.env
.env.*
*.pfx
*.snk
*_rsa
*.pem
